[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Understanding Props and Export Let in Svelte"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"In Svelte, components are the building blocks of your application. To make components reusable and dynamic, you need a way to pass data from parent components down to child components. This is achieved using props, which are declared in Svelte using the export keyword combined with the let keyword."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Declaring Props with export let"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Unlike frameworks like React or Vue, Svelte does not use a special arguments object for component properties. Instead, Svelte uses standard JavaScript variable declarations prefixed with the export keyword. This tells Svelte that the variable can be accessed from outside the component."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"<script>
  export let name = "World";
  export let age = 30;
</script>

<p>Hello {name}! You are {age} years old.</p>"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Passing Props from a Parent Component"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Once a property is exported in a child component, you can pass values to it from a parent component using standard HTML attribute syntax."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"<script>
  import UserCard from "./UserCard.svelte";
</script>

<UserCard name="Alice" age="25" />"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Key Concepts to Remember"},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Default values: You can assign a default value directly during declaration, such as export let theme = "light"."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Reactivity: Props are reactive. If the parent updates the prop value, the child component automatically updates to reflect the new value."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Reserved words: Do not use reserved JavaScript or Svelte keywords as prop names."}]
